## 1. Product Overview

Namma Space is an indoor digital-twin system. It converts captured visual information of a physical indoor environment into a 3D digital representation and presents it through an interactive web application.

A user opens a website and explores the reconstructed environment in the browser, without installing specialized software.

The product is being built for the **Namma Space Techfest competition at IIT Bombay**.

## 2. Background

A **digital twin** is a digital representation of a real-world object or place. In this project, the "object" is an indoor space. The digital version is built from data collected from the real one and can be viewed and interacted with on a computer.

The project follows this path:

1. Start with a real indoor environment.
2. Capture visual information about it (video and/or images).
3. Reconstruct the environment digitally in 3D.
4. Make the result accessible through a web browser.

The fidelity of the result depends on the captured data, the reconstruction method, and the optimization applied for web delivery. This document makes no claims about reconstruction accuracy or quality. Quality will be assessed on the actual output (see [Success Metrics](#26-success-metrics)).

## 3. Problem Statement

A physical indoor space is difficult to explore remotely when users cannot visit the location.

The project aims to create a digital representation of the space that lets users visually explore the environment through a web browser. The representation should preserve the important spatial and visual characteristics of the captured environment.

## 4. Product Vision

Provide an accessible, browser-based digital twin of an indoor environment that users can explore interactively.

The Round 1 system should be built as a foundation that can later support:

- Points of Interest (POIs)
- Search
- Navigation
- Pathfinding
- Additional spatial information

> These are **future capabilities**. They are **not** Round 1 functionality.

